Sloppy typing! Your host actually seems to be at <URL:http://www.utk.edu>.  But who am
I to point a stubby finger?

I can't point you to any good guidelines on this rather touchy subject, but I have a few
comments:

Having come from a small Jesuit college, I have little sympathy for those that feel they've
a right to determine the content of someone else's server.  Tuition pays for education, not
the keys to the campus.  The somewhat restrictive atmosphere at my college has not directly
affected my morals today, but it did provide a foundation for analysing my beliefs and
actions; for that I'm thankful.

It occurs to me that the problem schools are having with restricting newsgroup access and
server content (remember CMU?) is that they've failed to put forth their policies in the context
of overall guidelines regarding academic integrity and student/faculty conduct.  If you wouldn't
permit a student to publish pornography in the school newspaper, why must you allow it on a
Web server?  It seems like such a simple concept, yet I can't recall anyone stating it as such.

I hope this helps in determining and implementing your own policies. You've got a tough row
to hoe any way you look at it.  I _do_ think you'll have a much easier time of it, though, if you
develop a policy that is meaningful to your institution rather than the blanket adoption of
someone else's rules.

